# The header is two pointers wide on both runtimes and the two pointers are in opposite order,
# which is why "one pointer back from the data" is a MethodTable in one place and a monitor slot
# in the other. Coordinates picked so the two strips sit directly above each other and the offset
# columns line up by eye even though the cells are different widths in bytes.

title The word before an object, on two runtimes

note code 40 70 920 60 "object boxed = new Point { X = 0x11111111, Y = 0x22222222 };" "Column 0 is where AddrOfPinnedObject points."

box clrname 40 170 220 90 "osx-arm64" "CoreCLR" "eight byte words"
strip clr 280 170 170 90 "-16|sync block|0x0" "-8|MethodTable|0x301b41280" "0|X and Y|packed" "+8|padding|0x0"

box mononame 40 290 220 90 "browser-wasm" "Mono" "four byte words"
strip mono 280 290 170 90 "-8|MonoVTable|0x2f5e180" "-4|monitor|0x0" "0|X|0x11111111" "+4|Y|0x22222222"

arrow clr.1 mono.0 "the type pointer"

note ask 40 430 440 130 "The code reads one pointer" "back from the data. On" "CoreCLR that is the" "MethodTable, which is what" "a layout lesson wants."
note got 520 430 440 130 "Mono puts the same pointer" "one slot earlier. One back" "is the monitor slot, and" "it reads as zero. Nothing" "throws."

note count 40 590 920 90 "51 checks in the page. 2 throw. 49 return a value." "16 of the 49 describe the browser, not the runtime the book teaches." "Four of those are silent: this read, GC.Collect, a finalizer, a listener."

size 1000 700
